  "step_1": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the lottery that is more right-skewed, meaning a greater portion of the probability is assigned to unfavorable outcomes compared to favorable ones. In this context, 'unfavorable' outcomes are more negative (larger losses), and 'favorable' are less negative or zero. For Lottery A: 20% chance of -4000 (more unfavorable), 80% chance of 0 (neutral/favorable). For Lottery B: 25% chance of -3000 (less unfavorable than -4000), 75% chance of 0. To assess skewness: Lottery A has a lower probability (20%) on a more negative outcome, while Lottery B has a higher probability (25%) on a less negative outcome. Thus, Lottery B assigns more probability to a relatively unfavorable outcome (-3000) than Lottery A does to its unfavorable outcome (-4000), but -4000 is worse. However, in terms of skewness, right-skew in losses would imply higher probability on worse outcomes. Here, Lottery B has a higher probability (25%) of a loss than A (20%), but the loss in B is smaller. So, skewness comparison is non-trivial. Still, we can compute the probability-weighted direction: A has a rarer but larger loss. Typically, a lottery with a small chance of a large loss is considered left-skewed (in loss domain), while a higher chance of moderate loss is more right-skewed. Thus, Lottery B may be considered more right-skewed than A. The reason would prefer B if it is more right-skewed. However, this depends on the definition—here, since B has a higher probability of any loss, it could be seen as more right-skewed in the loss domain. So the reason could apply and would likely prefer B.",
