  "step_3": "The decision reason is based on skewness, specifically preferring the more right-skewed lottery (higher probability on unfavorable outcomes). In this case, Lottery B is more right-skewed if we define right-skew as higher probability of loss (even if smaller). The reason would prefer B. However, the individual seems to prefer A, which has lower probability of loss, and they justify this by rejecting a higher loss probability (which B has). Thus, their reasoning goes against the skewness reason. Moreover, the individual does not mention any concept related to distribution shape, skewness, or concentration of probability on unfavorable outcomes. Instead, they focus on the trade-off between probability and size of loss, showing a risk-averse preference for lower loss probability. There is no indication that they assessed which lottery has a more skewed distribution. The information they considered (loss amount and loss probability trade-off) overlaps partially with the inputs to skewness (probabilities and outcomes), but the rule they apply—avoid higher loss probability—is opposite to the skewness reason, which would favor the more right-skewed (higher loss probability) lottery. Therefore, the individual’s justification contradicts the decision reason. The reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

